# Fibers and Textiles
#
# A timeline ladder from wild flax and South Asian cotton through knotted records and punched-card looms to nylon and aramid, with the chemistry each fiber runs on.

#>materials_fibers
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!materials
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!intro
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Fibers and Textiles"
 ~#claim: "Twisted plant and animal fibers are among the oldest engineered materials, and the looms that wove them stored patterns on punched cards long before computers."
 ~#proof: "Kvavadze and Bergfjord on the disputed Dzudzuana fibers, Moulherat for Mehrgarh cotton, Locke and Urton for khipu, Essinger for the loom card lineage, Morton and Hearle for fiber chemistry."
 ~#door: "Start at the disputed Dzudzuana fibers, then follow the cards from Bouchon to Hollerith."
 ~#audience: "makers, historians of technology, and readers new to materials"
 continuity: "Fibers and Textiles | punched cards"
 cold_open: "Twisted fiber is one of our oldest materials, and the looms that wove it kept patterns on punched cards."
}

